---
export const prerender = true;

import Base from "@layouts/Base.astro";
import Button from "@components/ui/Button.astro";
import AudioButton from "@components/course/AudioButton";
import {
  ArrowRight,
  Repeat,
  Eye,
  AlertTriangle,
  CheckCircle2,
  XCircle,
  Sparkles,
} from "lucide-astro";

const lang = "en";
const tkey = "course/past-tenses/lesson-5" as const;
---

<Base
  lang={lang}
  tkey={tkey}
  title="Lesson 5: Used to / Would — The Habitual Past | NY English Teacher"
  description="The English equivalent of solía and the imperfecto for old habits. used to vs. would, the traps that trip Mexican speakers, and the drill that fixes it."
>
  <!-- Hero -->
  <section class="bg-gradient-to-b from-slate-900 to-emerald-950 pt-28 pb-16 md:pt-36 md:pb-20">
    <div class="site-container mx-auto px-4 text-white">
      <div class="max-w-3xl">
        <div class="flex items-center gap-2 text-emerald-400 text-sm font-semibold mb-3">
          <Repeat class="w-4 h-4" />
          Lesson 5 of 6
        </div>
        <h1
          class="text-3xl md:text-5xl font-bold text-white mb-4 leading-tight"
          style="font-family: 'Noto Sans KR', sans-serif;"
        >
          Used to / Would
        </h1>
        <p class="text-2xl text-emerald-300 italic font-light mb-4">It was a habit. It was repeated. It was who I was back then.</p>
        <p class="text-lg text-slate-300 max-w-2xl leading-relaxed">
          Spanish has <em>solía</em> and the <em>imperfecto</em> for the way things <em>used to be</em>.
          English has two structures — <strong>used to</strong> and habitual <strong>would</strong> — and they don't behave the same.
          Mistake them and you sound flat (everything in Past Simple) or unintentionally conditional ("I would going" / "I would be"). Master them and your past stories get texture.
        </p>
        <div class="mt-6 flex flex-wrap gap-3">
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-emerald-500/20 text-emerald-300 text-sm font-medium">
            <Repeat class="w-3.5 h-3.5" /> The Habitual Loop
          </span>
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-emerald-500/20 text-emerald-300 text-sm font-medium">
            <Sparkles class="w-3.5 h-3.5" /> Two Forms, One Idea
          </span>
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-emerald-500/20 text-emerald-300 text-sm font-medium">
            <AlertTriangle class="w-3.5 h-3.5" /> Spanish-Speaker Traps
          </span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Section 1: The Mental Picture -->
  <section class="py-16 md:py-20 bg-white" id="section-1">
    <div class="site-container mx-auto px-4">
      <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto">
        <div class="flex items-center gap-3 mb-2">
          <span class="flex items-center justify-center w-8 h-8 rounded-lg bg-emerald-500 text-white text-sm font-bold">1</span>
          <h2 class="text-2xl md:text-3xl font-bold text-slate-900">The Mental Picture</h2>
        </div>
        <p class="text-slate-500 mb-8 ml-11">Before any grammar. Just the image.</p>

        <div class="space-y-6 text-lg text-slate-700 leading-relaxed">
          <p>
            Past Simple is a snapshot. Past Continuous is a paused frame. Past Perfect is a layer below another past event. <strong>Used to / would is a loop on repeat.</strong>
          </p>
          <p>
            Picture a routine you had ten years ago. Not one specific morning — the whole shape of mornings, replayed over and over. Coffee at 6am. Same news on the radio. Same walk to the train. The brain doesn't store each instance — it stores the pattern. <strong>That pattern is what these two forms describe.</strong>
          </p>

          <div class="rounded-2xl border-2 border-emerald-300 bg-emerald-50 p-8 my-8">
            <div class="flex items-center gap-3 mb-4">
              <Eye class="w-6 h-6 text-emerald-700" />
              <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-slate-900">Your Mental Image</h3>
            </div>
            <p class="text-slate-800 mb-3">A loop. A pattern. A repeated scene from <em>back then</em> that doesn't happen anymore.</p>
            <p class="text-slate-700 text-base">If the action <em>repeated</em>, or the situation <em>persisted</em>, in a past period that's now closed — you want this lesson's structures, not plain Past Simple.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            The contrast that matters most: <strong>Past Simple is one event</strong> ("Last Sunday I called my grandmother"). <strong>Used to / would is the habit</strong> ("Every Sunday, I used to call her" / "Every Sunday, I would call her"). Same verb. Same person. Different shape of time.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Section 2: The Two Forms -->
  <section class="py-16 md:py-20 bg-slate-50" id="section-2">
    <div class="site-container mx-auto px-4">
      <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto">
        <div class="flex items-center gap-3 mb-2">
          <span class="flex items-center justify-center w-8 h-8 rounded-lg bg-emerald-500 text-white text-sm font-bold">2</span>
          <h2 class="text-2xl md:text-3xl font-bold text-slate-900">Two Forms. One Idea. Different Rules.</h2>
        </div>
        <p class="text-slate-500 mb-8 ml-11"><em>Used to</em> and habitual <em>would</em> both describe the past loop. But they don't overlap completely.</p>

        <div class="grid md:grid-cols-2 gap-6">
          <div class="bg-white rounded-2xl border-2 border-emerald-300 p-7">
            <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-slate-900 mb-2">used to + V</h3>
            <p class="text-sm text-emerald-700 font-mono mb-3">Works for actions AND states.</p>
            <p class="text-slate-700 text-sm mb-4">The flexible one. Covers anything that was repeatedly true or repeatedly done in the past.</p>
            <div class="bg-emerald-50 rounded-lg p-3 border border-emerald-200 text-sm space-y-2">
              <div class="flex items-center gap-2">
                <p class="flex-1"><strong>State:</strong> "I <strong>used to live</strong> in Guadalajara." (a situation that persisted)</p>
                <AudioButton client:load text="I used to live in Guadalajara." lang="en-US" size="sm" />
              </div>
              <div class="flex items-center gap-2">
                <p class="flex-1"><strong>Action:</strong> "I <strong>used to play</strong> soccer every Saturday." (a habit)</p>
                <AudioButton client:load text="I used to play soccer every Saturday." lang="en-US" size="sm" />
              </div>
              <div class="flex items-center gap-2">
                <p class="flex-1"><strong>Feeling:</strong> "She <strong>used to love</strong> chocolate." (an old preference)</p>
                <AudioButton client:load text="She used to love chocolate." lang="en-US" size="sm" />
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="bg-white rounded-2xl border-2 border-amber-400 p-7">
            <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-slate-900 mb-2">would + V</h3>
            <p class="text-sm text-amber-700 font-mono mb-3">Works for repeated ACTIONS only.</p>
            <p class="text-slate-700 text-sm mb-4">The restricted one. Use it for things that <em>happened</em> repeatedly — but never for states like <em>live</em>, <em>have</em>, <em>be</em>, <em>love</em>.</p>
            <div class="bg-amber-50 rounded-lg p-3 border border-amber-200 text-sm space-y-2">
              <div class="flex items-center gap-2">
                <p class="flex-1">✓ "Every Sunday, I <strong>would call</strong> my grandmother."</p>
                <AudioButton client:load text="Every Sunday, I would call my grandmother." lang="en-US" size="sm" />
              </div>
              <div class="flex items-center gap-2">
                <p class="flex-1">✓ "When we were kids, we <strong>would walk</strong> to school together."</p>
                <AudioButton client:load text="When we were kids, we would walk to school together." lang="en-US" size="sm" />
              </div>
              <p>✗ <span class="text-red-600">"I would live in Guadalajara"</span> — sounds like a conditional. Use <em>used to live</em>.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="mt-8 rounded-2xl bg-slate-900 text-white p-7">
          <p class="text-emerald-300 text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-wider mb-2">The shortcut rule</p>
          <p class="text-lg leading-relaxed">
            If the verb is something <em>you do</em> (call, walk, run, drink, ask), both <strong>used to</strong> and <strong>would</strong> work.<br />
            If the verb is something <em>you are</em> or <em>you have</em> (live, be, love, know, own), only <strong>used to</strong> works.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Section 3: Forms in detail (negative, question) -->
  <section class="py-16 md:py-20 bg-white" id="section-3">
    <div class="site-container mx-auto px-4">
      <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto">
        <div class="flex items-center gap-3 mb-2">
          <span class="flex items-center justify-center w-8 h-8 rounded-lg bg-emerald-500 text-white text-sm font-bold">3</span>
          <h2 class="text-2xl md:text-3xl font-bold text-slate-900">Negative & Question Forms</h2>
        </div>
        <p class="text-slate-500 mb-8 ml-11">This is where Spanish speakers slip — the "d" drops off when you add <em>did</em>.</p>

        <div class="space-y-5">
          <div class="rounded-xl border-2 border-emerald-300 bg-emerald-50/60 p-6">
            <p class="text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-wider text-emerald-700 mb-2">Positive</p>
            <div class="flex items-center gap-2">
              <p class="text-slate-800 flex-1">"I <strong>used to</strong> drink coffee every morning."</p>
              <AudioButton client:load text="I used to drink coffee every morning." lang="en-US" size="sm" />
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="rounded-xl border-2 border-amber-300 bg-amber-50/60 p-6">
            <p class="text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-wider text-amber-700 mb-2">Negative (notice — no <em>'d'</em> after <em>didn't</em>)</p>
            <div class="flex items-center gap-2">
              <p class="text-slate-800 flex-1">"I <strong>didn't use to</strong> drink coffee."</p>
              <AudioButton client:load text="I didn't use to drink coffee." lang="en-US" size="sm" />
            </div>
            <p class="text-sm text-slate-500 mt-1">Not "didn't <s>used</s> to." The <em>did</em> already carries the past — the verb drops to <em>use</em>.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="rounded-xl border-2 border-amber-300 bg-amber-50/60 p-6">
            <p class="text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-wider text-amber-700 mb-2">Question</p>
            <div class="flex items-center gap-2">
              <p class="text-slate-800 flex-1">"<strong>Did</strong> you <strong>use to</strong> drink coffee?"</p>
              <AudioButton client:load text="Did you use to drink coffee?" lang="en-US" size="sm" />
            </div>
            <p class="text-sm text-slate-500 mt-1">Same rule. <em>Did</em> first, then <em>use to</em> (no 'd').</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="mt-8 bg-slate-50 rounded-xl border border-slate-200 p-5">
          <p class="text-sm text-slate-700">
            <strong class="text-slate-900">For <em>would</em>:</strong> the negative ("wouldn't") and question forms ("Would you call her every Sunday?") usually <em>aren't</em> used for the habitual sense — they sound more like the conditional. Stick to <strong>didn't use to</strong> and <strong>Did you use to</strong> for negatives and questions about old habits.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Section 4: The Mexican Traps -->
  <section class="py-16 md:py-20 bg-slate-50" id="section-4">
    <div class="site-container mx-auto px-4">
      <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto">
        <div class="flex items-center gap-3 mb-2">
          <span class="flex items-center justify-center w-8 h-8 rounded-lg bg-emerald-500 text-white text-sm font-bold">4</span>
          <h2 class="text-2xl md:text-3xl font-bold text-slate-900">Four Traps that Trip Mexican Speakers</h2>
        </div>
        <p class="text-slate-500 mb-8 ml-11">These four account for almost every "used to / would" mistake in real conversation.</p>

        <div class="space-y-4">
          <div class="bg-white rounded-2xl border-2 border-red-200 p-6">
            <div class="flex items-start gap-3 mb-2">
              <XCircle class="w-5 h-5 text-red-600 shrink-0 mt-1" />
              <div>
                <p class="text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-wider text-red-700">Trap 1 — Confusing habitual <em>would</em> with conditional <em>would</em></p>
                <p class="text-slate-800 mt-1">"I would be a teacher when I was young."</p>
                <p class="text-sm text-slate-500 mt-2">This sounds <em>conditional</em> ("would be" if X). For an old state, use <strong>used to be</strong>: "I <strong>used to be</strong> a teacher when I was young."</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="bg-white rounded-2xl border-2 border-red-200 p-6">
            <div class="flex items-start gap-3 mb-2">
              <XCircle class="w-5 h-5 text-red-600 shrink-0 mt-1" />
              <div>
                <p class="text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-wider text-red-700">Trap 2 — Adding 'd' after didn't</p>
                <p class="text-slate-800 mt-1">"I didn't <s>used</s> to like coffee."</p>
                <p class="text-sm text-slate-500 mt-2">The <em>did</em> already carries the past. Drop the 'd': "I <strong>didn't use to</strong> like coffee."</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="bg-white rounded-2xl border-2 border-red-200 p-6">
            <div class="flex items-start gap-3 mb-2">
              <XCircle class="w-5 h-5 text-red-600 shrink-0 mt-1" />
              <div>
                <p class="text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-wider text-red-700">Trap 3 — Mapping every <em>imperfecto</em> to <em>used to</em></p>
                <p class="text-slate-800 mt-1">"It was raining when I arrived" ≠ "It used to rain when I arrived."</p>
                <p class="text-sm text-slate-500 mt-2"><em>Imperfecto</em> covers BOTH habitual ("I used to walk to school") AND in-progress ("I was walking to school when…"). The choice in English depends on whether it's a <em>habit</em> (used to / would) or a <em>scene mid-action</em> (Past Continuous — Lesson 2).</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="bg-white rounded-2xl border-2 border-red-200 p-6">
            <div class="flex items-start gap-3 mb-2">
              <XCircle class="w-5 h-5 text-red-600 shrink-0 mt-1" />
              <div>
                <p class="text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-wider text-red-700">Trap 4 — Using -ing after <em>used to</em></p>
                <p class="text-slate-800 mt-1">"I used to <s>working</s> at Telmex."</p>
                <p class="text-sm text-slate-500 mt-2"><strong>Used to + base verb.</strong> "I used to <strong>work</strong> at Telmex." (The form <em>be used to + V-ing</em> means something completely different — being <em>accustomed</em> — and isn't past at all.)</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Section 5: Used to vs Past Simple -->
  <section class="py-16 md:py-20 bg-white" id="section-5">
    <div class="site-container mx-auto px-4">
      <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto">
        <div class="flex items-center gap-3 mb-2">
          <span class="flex items-center justify-center w-8 h-8 rounded-lg bg-emerald-500 text-white text-sm font-bold">5</span>
          <h2 class="text-2xl md:text-3xl font-bold text-slate-900">"Used to" vs. Past Simple — The Choose-It Drill</h2>
        </div>
        <p class="text-slate-500 mb-8 ml-11">Both work in many sentences. The choice is about <em>shape</em> — repeated pattern vs. single event.</p>

        <div class="bg-emerald-50 rounded-2xl border-2 border-emerald-300 p-6 md:p-8">
          <div class="space-y-4">
            <div class="grid md:grid-cols-2 gap-3 items-start pb-3 border-b border-emerald-200">
              <p class="text-slate-700">"___ I ___ in Monterrey for three years."</p>
              <div class="flex items-start gap-2 text-sm">
                <div class="flex-1"><CheckCircle2 class="inline w-4 h-4 text-emerald-600" /> <strong>I lived</strong> — a closed period with a specific duration → Past Simple.</div>
                <AudioButton client:load text="I lived in Monterrey for three years." lang="en-US" size="sm" />
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="grid md:grid-cols-2 gap-3 items-start pb-3 border-b border-emerald-200">
              <p class="text-slate-700">"___ When I was a kid, I ___ in Monterrey."</p>
              <div class="flex items-start gap-2 text-sm">
                <div class="flex-1"><CheckCircle2 class="inline w-4 h-4 text-emerald-600" /> <strong>used to live</strong> — describing an old state with no duration ("when I was a kid" → vague past period).</div>
                <AudioButton client:load text="When I was a kid, I used to live in Monterrey." lang="en-US" size="sm" />
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="grid md:grid-cols-2 gap-3 items-start pb-3 border-b border-emerald-200">
              <p class="text-slate-700">"Last Sunday, I ___ my grandmother."</p>
              <div class="flex items-start gap-2 text-sm">
                <div class="flex-1"><CheckCircle2 class="inline w-4 h-4 text-emerald-600" /> <strong>called</strong> — one specific event → Past Simple.</div>
                <AudioButton client:load text="Last Sunday, I called my grandmother." lang="en-US" size="sm" />
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="grid md:grid-cols-2 gap-3 items-start pb-3 border-b border-emerald-200">
              <p class="text-slate-700">"Every Sunday, I ___ my grandmother."</p>
              <div class="flex items-start gap-2 text-sm">
                <div class="flex-1"><CheckCircle2 class="inline w-4 h-4 text-emerald-600" /> <strong>used to call</strong> OR <strong>would call</strong> — the repeated habit. Both work because <em>call</em> is an action.</div>
                <div class="flex flex-col gap-1">
                  <AudioButton client:load text="Every Sunday, I used to call my grandmother." lang="en-US" size="sm" />
                  <AudioButton client:load text="Every Sunday, I would call my grandmother." lang="en-US" size="sm" />
                </div>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="grid md:grid-cols-2 gap-3 items-start pb-3 border-b border-emerald-200">
              <p class="text-slate-700">"She ___ coffee, but now she drinks tea."</p>
              <div class="flex items-start gap-2 text-sm">
                <div class="flex-1"><CheckCircle2 class="inline w-4 h-4 text-emerald-600" /> <strong>used to drink</strong> — past habit explicitly contrasted with the present → screams for <em>used to</em>.</div>
                <AudioButton client:load text="She used to drink coffee, but now she drinks tea." lang="en-US" size="sm" />
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="grid md:grid-cols-2 gap-3 items-start">
              <p class="text-slate-700">"Back then, we ___ broke."</p>
              <div class="flex items-start gap-2 text-sm">
                <div class="flex-1"><CheckCircle2 class="inline w-4 h-4 text-emerald-600" /> <strong>used to be</strong> — state. <em>Would be</em> is wrong here (sounds conditional).</div>
                <AudioButton client:load text="Back then, we used to be broke." lang="en-US" size="sm" />
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Wrap-up -->
  <section class="py-16 md:py-20 bg-slate-50">
    <div class="site-container mx-auto px-4">
      <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto">
        <div class="text-center mb-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl md:text-3xl font-bold text-slate-900 mb-3">Lesson 5 Wrap-Up</h2>
          <p class="text-lg text-slate-600">Take the five rules below into your next conversation.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="bg-white rounded-2xl border border-slate-200 p-8 mb-10">
          <ul class="space-y-3 text-slate-700">
            <li class="flex gap-3"><CheckCircle2 class="w-5 h-5 text-emerald-600 shrink-0 mt-0.5" /><span><strong>Used to / would = the past loop.</strong> Repeated actions or old states. The English version of <em>solía</em> and habitual <em>imperfecto</em>.</span></li>
            <li class="flex gap-3"><CheckCircle2 class="w-5 h-5 text-emerald-600 shrink-0 mt-0.5" /><span><strong>Used to</strong> = states + actions. <strong>Would</strong> = actions only. "Would be" / "would live" / "would have" almost always sound conditional, not habitual.</span></li>
            <li class="flex gap-3"><CheckCircle2 class="w-5 h-5 text-emerald-600 shrink-0 mt-0.5" /><span><strong>After <em>didn't</em> and <em>did</em>, drop the 'd'.</strong> "I <em>didn't use to</em>", "<em>Did you use to</em>".</span></li>
            <li class="flex gap-3"><CheckCircle2 class="w-5 h-5 text-emerald-600 shrink-0 mt-0.5" /><span><strong>Always base verb after <em>used to</em>.</strong> Never -ing in the past sense ("used to working" is wrong for past habit).</span></li>
            <li class="flex gap-3"><CheckCircle2 class="w-5 h-5 text-emerald-600 shrink-0 mt-0.5" /><span><strong>Imperfecto splits into two English tenses.</strong> Habit → used to / would. Mid-action scene → Past Continuous (Lesson 2). Don't conflate them.</span></li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="text-center">
          <p class="text-slate-600 mb-6">Final lesson: <strong class="text-slate-900">The Story Flow Map</strong> — combining all the past tenses you've learned and choosing each one in real time, mid-sentence.</p>
          <Button href="/en/course/past-tenses/lesson-6/" variant="primary" size="lg">
            Lesson 6: The Story Flow Map
            <ArrowRight class="w-5 h-5 ml-1" />
          </Button>
          <p class="text-sm text-slate-600 mt-6 max-w-xl mx-auto">
            Habitual past is one of the hardest patterns to install — knowing the rule isn't enough. The
            <a href="/en/course/past-tenses/practice-plan/" class="text-emerald-700 hover:text-emerald-800 underline underline-offset-2 font-medium">30-day practice plan</a>
            spells out the daily drills that move <em>used to</em> and <em>would</em> from page to mouth.
          </p>
          <div class="mt-6 flex flex-wrap gap-4 justify-center">
            <a
              href="/en/course/past-tenses/lesson-4/"
              class="text-sm text-slate-500 hover:text-emerald-700 underline underline-offset-2"
            >
              ← Lesson 4: Past Perfect
            </a>
            <span class="text-slate-300">|</span>
            <a
              href="/en/course/past-tenses/"
              class="text-sm text-emerald-700 hover:text-emerald-800 underline underline-offset-2"
            >
              Course overview
            </a>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify({
    "@context": "https://schema.org",
    "@type": "LearningResource",
    "name": "Lesson 5: Used to / Would — The Habitual Past",
    "description": "The English equivalent of Spanish solia and the imperfecto for old habits and states. Used to vs would, four Mexican-speaker traps, and a choose-it drill against Past Simple.",
    "learningResourceType": "Lesson",
    "educationalLevel": "Intermediate to Advanced",
    "inLanguage": "en",
    "isAccessibleForFree": true,
    "url": "https://www.nyenglishteacher.com/en/course/past-tenses/lesson-5/",
    "isPartOf": {
      "@type": "Course",
      "name": "Master English Past Tenses — Free Master Class",
      "url": "https://www.nyenglishteacher.com/en/course/past-tenses/"
    },
    "author": {
